    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The full suite is green (BUILD SUCCESS, 1 test, 0 failures).\n\nI implemented the email-uniqueness rule following the existing conventions in the codebase:\n\n1. **`DuplicateEmailException`** (new) \u2014 a `RuntimeException` mirroring `DuplicateTelephoneException`, documented as a 409 Conflict.\n\n2. **`ExceptionControllerAdvice`** \u2014 added an `@ExceptionHandler` that maps `DuplicateEmailException` to `409 Conflict`, reporting the `email` field, using the same `errorResponse(...)` helper the other business-rule handlers use.\n\n3. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.normalizeAndValidate`** \u2014 after normalizing the email to its canonical (lower-cased) form, it now calls a new `requireUniqueEmail(...)` check. That check skips the optional-and-blank case, then compares the incoming lower-cased email against every existing owner's normalized email (via the shared `EmailNormalizer`, so stored emails are compared case-insensitively too), throwing `DuplicateEmailException` on a match.\n\nThe acceptance test `coreRejectsDuplicateEmail` \u2014 create an owner with an email, then create a second with the same email \u2014 now returns 409 as required.",
